Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS)
Young Adult & Adolecence
Oligomenorrhoea/Amenorrhoea, Clinical hirsutism, Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound, Refractory acne

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-033
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, painful nodulocystic facial acne causing profound psychological distress, acute social withdrawal, and refusal to attend employment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a young adult experiencing a rapid, painful exacerbation of severe facial acne, superimposed on a background of worsening hirsutism and irregular menses, culminating in acute psychological decompensation and an inability to leave the home. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate the severity of the inflammatory skin lesions, exclude a spreading secondary bacterial infection (cellulitis), and provide immediate clinical validation and psychological stabilization prior to routine clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face is covered in agonizing, deep cysts that throb constantly, my facial hair is getting worse, and I cannot face the public at work looking like this."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe, acute flare of refractory acne vulgaris, characterized by deep, painful nodulocystic lesions on the jawline and cheeks. This skin exacerbation occurs on a background of chronic oligomenorrhoea (cycles varying from 45 to 90 days) and worsening clinical hyperandrogenism, specifically moderate hirsutism on the chin and upper lip, and thinning scalp hair (androgenic alopecia). The patient also notes recent central weight gain and a darkening of the skin on the back of the neck (acanthosis nigricans). The acute physical pain of the cysts and the profound psychosocial distress regarding their appearance have triggered severe mood lability and an acute refusal to attend work.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently homebound due to severe psychosocial distress and localized facial pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute psychological distress; occupational impairment due to social withdrawal; no active self-harm ideation, but severe depressive symptoms present.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears highly distressed, avoiding eye contact, and attempting to obscure the lower face with their hands.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals multiple large (1-2cm), tender, erythematous nodulocystic lesions distributed predominantly along the jawline and lower cheeks. There is severe seborrhoea. Coarse terminal hairs are visibly present on the chin and upper lip. Examination of the posterior neck reveals hyperpigmented, velvety plaques consistent with acanthosis nigricans, indicating underlying insulin resistance. The clinical picture perfectly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of oligomenorrhoea, clinical hirsutism, refractory acne, and a high likelihood of finding polycystic ovaries on a future transvaginal ultrasound. There is no spreading cellulitis or systemic toxicity.
Environmental Observations: Multiple over-the-counter acne washes and bleaching creams are visible, heavily irritating the skin.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Exacerbation of Refractory Acne secondary to Suspected Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) - Status: Acute physical and psychological exacerbation
Differentials:

Late-Onset Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to its rarity, though it presents similarly with hyperandrogenism and requires a fasting 17-hydroxyprogesterone level to definitively exclude.

Cushing's Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of purple abdominal striae, proximal muscle weakness, and the characteristic supraclavicular fat pads, though weight gain is present.

Simple Acne Vulgaris - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of systemic hyperandrogenic signs (hirsutism, alopecia) and severe menstrual irregularity, indicating a systemic endocrine etiology rather than an isolated dermatological one.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Severe psychosocial impact and occupational impairment)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, painful flare of nodulocystic acne driving a severe psychological crisis. The physical findings of hirsutism, acanthosis nigricans, and oligomenorrhoea strongly indicate underlying severe hyperandrogenemia and insulin resistance, classic for PCOS. Delaying clinical assessment until the next day would have prolonged severe physical pain, unmanaged psychological distress, and further entrenched the patient's acute social withdrawal. The immediate after-hours home visit allowed for the safe exclusion of a spreading facial soft-tissue infection, the initiation of systemic antibiotics to calm the severe acute skin inflammation, and crucial medical validation that the symptoms are caused by a treatable systemic hormone imbalance, instantly reducing the patient's profound guilt and distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Doxycycline 100mg PO daily to rapidly target the deep inflammatory cystic acne component. Advised the regular GP to order comprehensive PCOS serology (Free Androgen Index, Testosterone, LH/FSH ratio, fasting glucose/insulin) and a pelvic ultrasound to confirm the diagnosis. Provided a medical certificate to excuse the patient from work for the next 3 days due to the acute inflammatory flare.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce the aggressive skin inflammation, completely cease all harsh over-the-counter scrubs and bleaching creams, acknowledging that attacking the skin surface worsens the redness and pain of deep cysts. To begin managing the underlying hormonal driver, substitute all sugary beverages with water starting tomorrow, actively reducing the insulin spikes that directly trigger the ovaries to produce excess testosterone.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 7 days to initiate a formal PCOS management plan, which may include anti-androgen oral contraceptives (e.g., Diane-35) or Spironolactone to treat the root cause of the acne and hirsutism.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if a facial cyst becomes rapidly swollen, hot, bright red, and begins spreading towards the eye, or if a high fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically suspected PCOS; definitive proof requires specific fasting blood tests and an ultrasound.
Clinical Course: The oral antibiotics will begin to reduce the painful swelling of the cysts within a week, but the long-term cure for this acne requires hormonal medications prescribed by your GP to block the excess testosterone.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the redness around any of the cysts spreads rapidly across your cheek, if your eye swells shut, or if you develop a high fever and shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your severe acne and irregular periods are not a hygiene issue; they are caused by a hormonal imbalance that drives your body to produce too much testosterone. The antibiotic prescribed today will act as an anti-inflammatory to shrink the painful cysts, but you must see your GP to start medication that fixes the underlying hormone issue.
Actionable Steps: Stop using all rough physical scrubs; wash your face gently with your hands only. Take the doxycycline with a full glass of water and a meal to prevent stomach upset.

